#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189095} :activity/id #uuid "35cac34d-08b2-58e8-9601-d836880ca5df" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048466 :topic/id #uuid "a1f79e8d-032f-59f2-bf48-1433971eb75e" :topic/math-academy-id 3856} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189081 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188276 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188211 :question/id #uuid "136ceff6-b85b-5686-a69b-5d39ff70d3dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-9191"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1892c89c-8343-57ff-8c7e-e6edb35bb7c6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049412}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188537}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Expected Value of a Discrete Random Variable From a Joint Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "24560547-2ea6-5ad9-9bbb-ff6eb26fcf18" :step/math-academy-id 34281 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189120}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189095 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189371 :tutorial/content "To compute the expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ given a joint mass function $f(x,y)$, we must first find its marginal mass function $f_X$. Once the marginal mass function is known, we can then compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n x_i \\, f_X(x_i)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$, shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ |\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Since we're only interested in the expected value of $X$, let's compute the row totals only.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.20}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.80}$ |\n\nTherefore, the marginal mass function of $X$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.20$ | $0.80$ |\n\nWe can now compute the expected value of $X$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}f_{X}(x_{i}) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 0.2 + 2 \\cdot 0.8 \\\\\n&= 0.2 + 1.6 \\\\\n&= 1.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = 1.8\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0512a52f-ccb2-56a2-b436-f49ec9cb4916" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6253 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3c190be0-696c-59a8-9758-3931bd0f72ac" :step/math-academy-id 37912 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189081}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189115 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188296 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188003 :question/id #uuid "f32f96bc-b02a-54fe-b370-8a384c6ae67e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10102"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7447ccfe-83a7-59d4-8386-c74a3ab47b89" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049801}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188564}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Expected Value of a Continuous Random Variable From a Joint Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "595885fa-5146-5a3a-8643-30f924026039" :step/math-academy-id 38102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189120 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189382 :tutorial/content "To compute the expected value of a *continuous* random variable $X$ given a joint density function $f(x,y)$, we must first find its marginal density function $f_X$. Once the marginal density function is known, we can then compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty x \\cdot f_X(x)\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "9ed12512-4300-57a6-b68d-2cacfa2f3488" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6320 :tutorial/title "Continuous Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "daffc16b-1f95-542f-8a1a-225169b2cb6e" :step/math-academy-id 38236 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189115}}] :activity/title "Computing Expected Values From Joint Distributions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186188451}]}
